  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "1. A weight/activation tile sits in GDDR6 (32 GB, 512 GB/s).",
      "focus": [
        "gddr6"
      ],
      "dot": "gddr6",
      "explode": 0.4,
      "drill": null,
      "stage": "hbm"
    },
    {
      "text": "2. The 2D NoC (NoC0 moving down/right, NoC1 moving up/left) routes the tile's packet across the mesh toward the target Tensix core.",
      "focus": [
        "noc-fabric"
      ],
      "dot": "noc-fabric",
      "explode": 0.5,
      "stage": "noc"
    },
    {
      "text": "3. The packet reaches a Tensix core and is handed to its local 1.5 MB L1 SRAM.",
      "focus": [
        "tensix-core"
      ],
      "dot": "tensix-core",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "explode": 0.6,
      "stage": "l1"
    },
    {
      "text": "4. The unpacker reads the packet into L1 circular buffers and converts it into tile layout.",
      "focus": [
        "unpacker"
      ],
      "dot": "unpacker",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "unpack"
    },
    {
      "text": "5. The FPU (tile matrix engine) multiplies 32×32 tiles — ~4 TFLOPS/core at 1 GHz, the Tensix's matmul engine.",
      "focus": [
        "fpu"
      ],
      "dot": "fpu",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "tensor-core"
    },
    {
      "text": "6. The SFPU (wide SIMD vector engine) applies elementwise/transcendental ops — GELU, exp, softmax — on the FPU's output.",
      "focus": [
        "sfpu"
      ],
      "dot": "sfpu",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "epilogue"
    },
    {
      "text": "7. The packer repackitizes the results from L1 back into NoC packet format.",
      "focus": [
        "packer"
      ],
      "dot": "packer",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "pack"
    },
    {
      "text": "8. The packet leaves through one of the Tensix's 2 NoC routers toward the next core in the dataflow graph, or back to GDDR6.",
      "focus": [
        "tensix-router"
      ],
      "dot": "tensix-router",
      "drill": null,
      "stage": "writeback"
    },
    {
      "text": "9. For multi-chip scale-out, packets can also exit over one of the 10×400G Ethernet ports to another Blackhole die (e.g. in a Galaxy system).",
      "focus": [
        "ethernet-port"
      ],
      "dot": "ethernet-port",
      "stage": "ethernet"
    },
    {
      "text": "10. The host CPU talks to the card over PCIe 5.0 x16 to launch kernels and stream tensors; the ARC microcontroller manages boot and power.",
      "focus": [
        "pcie",
        "arc"
      ],
      "dot": "pcie",
      "stage": "host"
    }
  ],
